What is the JSON data representation of the SQL query to retrieve all European countries and their respective capitals from a database? 
Assuming there is a table named 'countries' with columns 'country' and 'capital', the JSON data representation would be:
{
  "SELECT": {
    "columns": ["country", "capital"],
    "from": "countries",
    "where": {
      "country": {
        "$in": [
          "Albania", "Andorra", "Austria", "Belarus", "Belgium", "Bosnia and Herzegovina", "Bulgaria",
          "Croatia", "Cyprus", "Czech Republic", "Denmark", "Estonia", "Finland", "France", "Germany",
          "Greece", "Hungary", "Iceland", "Ireland", "Italy", "Kosovo", "Latvia", "Liechtenstein", 
          "Lithuania", "Luxembourg", "Malta", "Moldova", "Monaco", "Montenegro", "Netherlands", 
          "North Macedonia", "Norway", "Poland", "Portugal", "Romania", "Russia", "San Marino", 
          "Serbia", "Slovakia", "Slovenia", "Spain", "Sweden", "Switzerland", "Ukraine", 
          "United Kingdom", "Vatican City"
        ]
      }
    }
  }
}
This JSON data can be used to communicate with APIs or other programs that accept data in JSON format.

The Rust compiler has a modular architecture consisting of multiple components, each responsible for a specific task. The compiler can be broadly divided into three major components: Frontend, Middle-end, and Backend.

The frontend component is responsible for parsing the source code, performing syntax and semantic analysis, and generating an abstract syntax tree (AST) representation of the program. This phase includes lexing, parsing, name resolution, type-checking, and macro expansion.

The middle-end component performs various optimization passes on the AST generated by the frontend. This includes data flow analysis, control flow analysis, and other optimization techniques. The output of the middle-end is a lower-level intermediate representation (IR) of the program that is closer to machine code.

The backend component generates the final machine code from the IR produced by the middle-end. This involves instruction selection, register allocation, and instruction scheduling. The output of the backend is platform-specific object code that can be linked to form a complete executable.

