I compiled the following code with the command "g++ -O3 test.cpp" and I expected the first branch to run faster because the compiler can inline the function call to "accu". However, I was surprised to see that the second branch, which calls "accu" through a function pointer stored in an array, actually runs faster. I don't understand why. I even checked the disassembly of the code using "objdump -DStTrR a.out > a.s", but that didn't explain the performance result. Can someone please explain why the second branch runs faster than the first branch?

```cpp
typedef void (*Fn)();

volatile long sum = 0;

inline void accu() {
    sum+=4;
}

static const Fn map[4] = {&accu, &accu, &accu, &accu};

int main(int argc, char** argv) {
    static const long N = 10000000L;
    if (argc == 1)
    {
        for (long i = 0; i < N; i++)
        {
            accu();
            accu();
            accu();
            accu();
        }
    }
    else
    {
        for (long i = 0; i < N; i++)
        {
            for (int j = 0; j < 4; j++)
                (*map[j])();
        }
    }
}
